WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:02.775
Now the injuries that come with fishing.

2
00:00:03.025 --> 00:00:04.625
Fish hooks.

3
00:00:04.875 --> 00:00:06.850
Most you can deal with.

4
00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:11.575
But a hook in or near the eye, or deep in a joint, stays where it is.

5
00:00:11.825 --> 00:00:13.750
Secure it and go in.

6
00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.875
Venomous spines: stingrays, catfish, lionfish.

7
00:00:18.125 --> 00:00:20.025
The pain is fierce.

8
00:00:20.275 --> 00:00:25.200
Soak the wound in water as hot as the person can stand without burning.

9
00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:27.200
Why hot water?

10
00:00:28.200 --> 00:00:30.300
Heat breaks down the venom.

11
00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:32.400
Jellyfish stings.

12
00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:35.475
Rinse with seawater, not fresh water.

13
00:00:35.725 --> 00:00:40.150
Fresh water fires off the stinging cells that haven’t gone off yet.

14
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:43.025
Cuts from fish, shells and coral.

15
00:00:43.275 --> 00:00:44.875
Clean them well.

16
00:00:45.125 --> 00:00:55.150
Warm Florida water carries Vibrio bacteria, which can cause a serious infection, especially in people with liver disease or weak immune systems.

17
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:57.150
And seasickness.

18
00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:01.150
Keep them on deck, in fresh air, looking at the horizon.

19
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.525
Below decks makes it worse.
